window.SICP_EX3 = [

  // ── Section 3.1: Assignment and Local State ───────────────────────────

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.1', html: '<p>An <em>accumulator</em> is a procedure that is called repeatedly with a single numeric argument and accumulates its arguments into a sum. Each time it is called, it returns the currently accumulated sum. Write a procedure <code>make-accumulator</code> that generates accumulators, each maintaining an independent sum. The input to <code>make-accumulator</code> should specify the initial value of the sum.</p><pre>(define A (make-accumulator 5))\n(A 10)  ; 15\n(A 10)  ; 25</pre>', code: '(define (make-accumulator n)\n  (lambda (amount)\n    (set! n (+ n amount))\n    n))\n\n(define A (make-accumulator 5))\n(A 10)  ; 15\n(A 10)  ; 25' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.2', html: '<p>In software-testing applications, it is useful to be able to count the number of times a given procedure is called during the course of a computation. Write a procedure <code>make-monitored</code> that takes as input a procedure <code>f</code> that itself takes one input. The result returned by <code>make-monitored</code> is a third procedure, say <code>mf</code>, that keeps track of the number of times it has been called by maintaining an internal counter. If the input to <code>mf</code> is the special symbol <code>how-many-calls?</code>, then <code>mf</code> returns the value of the counter. If the input is the special symbol <code>reset-count</code>, then <code>mf</code> resets the counter to zero. For any other input, <code>mf</code> returns the result of calling <code>f</code> on that input and increments the counter.</p>', code: '(define (make-monitored f)\n  (let ((count 0))\n    (lambda (x)\n      (cond ((eq? x \'how-many-calls?) count)\n            ((eq? x \'reset-count) (set! count 0))\n            (else (set! count (+ count 1))\n                  (f x))))))\n\n(define s (make-monitored sqrt))\n(s 100)             ; 10\n(s 81)              ; 9\n(s \'how-many-calls?) ; 2\n(s \'reset-count)\n(s \'how-many-calls?) ; 0' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.3', html: '<p>Modify the <code>make-account</code> procedure so that it creates password-protected accounts. That is, <code>make-account</code> should take a symbol as an additional argument, as in</p><pre>(define acc (make-account 100 \'secret-password))</pre><p>The resulting account object should process a request only if it is accompanied by the password with which the account was created, and should otherwise return a complaint:</p><pre>((acc \'secret-password \'withdraw) 40)  ; 60\n((acc \'some-other-password \'deposit) 50)  ; "Incorrect password"</pre>', code: '(define (make-account balance password)\n  (define (withdraw amount)\n    (if (>= balance amount)\n        (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n        "Insufficient funds"))\n  (define (deposit amount)\n    (set! balance (+ balance amount))\n    balance)\n  (define (dispatch pw m)\n    (if (not (eq? pw password))\n        (lambda (x) "Incorrect password")\n        (cond ((eq? m \'withdraw) withdraw)\n              ((eq? m \'deposit) deposit)\n              ((eq? m \'balance) balance)\n              (else (error "Unknown request" m)))))\n  dispatch)\n\n(define acc (make-account 100 \'secret-password))\n((acc \'secret-password \'withdraw) 40)         ; 60\n((acc \'some-other-password \'deposit) 50)      ; "Incorrect password"' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.4', html: '<p>Modify the <code>make-account</code> procedure of exercise 3.3 by adding another local state variable so that, if an account is accessed more than seven consecutive times with an incorrect password, it invokes the procedure <code>call-the-cops</code>.</p>', code: '(define (call-the-cops) (display "Calling the cops!"))\n\n(define (make-account balance password)\n  (let ((bad-count 0))\n    (define (withdraw amount)\n      (if (>= balance amount)\n          (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n          "Insufficient funds"))\n    (define (deposit amount)\n      (set! balance (+ balance amount))\n      balance)\n    (define (dispatch pw m)\n      (if (not (eq? pw password))\n          (begin\n            (set! bad-count (+ bad-count 1))\n            (if (> bad-count 7) (call-the-cops))\n            (lambda (x) "Incorrect password"))\n          (begin\n            (set! bad-count 0)\n            (cond ((eq? m \'withdraw) withdraw)\n                  ((eq? m \'deposit) deposit)\n                  ((eq? m \'balance) balance)\n                  (else (error "Unknown request" m))))))\n    dispatch))\n\n(define acc (make-account 100 \'secret))\n((acc \'wrong \'withdraw) 10)\n((acc \'wrong \'withdraw) 10)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.5', html: '<p>Monte Carlo integration is a method of estimating definite integrals by means of Monte Carlo simulation. Consider computing the area of a region of space described by a predicate P(x, y) that is true for points (x, y) in the region and false for points not in the region. Estimate the integral of P over a rectangle by choosing sample points (x, y) at random in the rectangle and testing P(x, y). The fraction of points that pass the test gives an estimate of the integral.</p><p>Implement Monte Carlo integration as a procedure <code>estimate-integral</code> that takes as arguments a predicate <code>P</code>, upper and lower bounds <code>x1</code>, <code>x2</code>, <code>y1</code>, <code>y2</code> for the rectangle, and the number of trials to perform. Use your procedure to estimate &pi; using the predicate that checks whether a point falls within a circle of radius 3 centered at (5, 7).</p>', code: '(define (random-in-range low high)\n  (let ((range (- high low)))\n    (+ low (* (random 1.0) range))))\n\n(define (monte-carlo trials experiment)\n  (define (iter trials-remaining trials-passed)\n    (cond ((= trials-remaining 0)\n           (/ trials-passed trials))\n          ((experiment)\n           (iter (- trials-remaining 1) (+ trials-passed 1)))\n          (else\n           (iter (- trials-remaining 1) trials-passed))))\n  (iter trials 0))\n\n(define (estimate-integral P x1 x2 y1 y2 trials)\n  (let ((area (* (- x2 x1) (- y2 y1))))\n    (* area\n       (monte-carlo trials\n                    (lambda ()\n                      (P (random-in-range x1 x2)\n                         (random-in-range y1 y2)))))))\n\n; Estimate pi: circle of radius 3 centered at (5,7)\n(define (in-circle? x y)\n  (<= (+ (expt (- x 5) 2) (expt (- y 7) 2)) 9))\n\n(estimate-integral in-circle? 2.0 8.0 4.0 10.0 10000)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.6', html: '<p>It is useful to be able to reset a random-number generator to produce a sequence starting from a given value. Design a new <code>rand</code> procedure that is called with an argument that is either the symbol <code>generate</code> or the symbol <code>reset</code>, and behaves as follows: <code>(rand \'generate)</code> produces a new random number; <code>((rand \'reset) &lt;new-value&gt;)</code> resets the internal state variable to the designated <code>&lt;new-value&gt;</code>. Thus, by resetting the state, one can generate repeatable sequences.</p>', code: '; Assume rand-update advances a pseudo-random state:\n(define (rand-update x)\n  (remainder (+ (* 1103515245 x) 12345) (expt 2 31)))\n\n(define rand\n  (let ((state 42))\n    (lambda (msg)\n      (cond ((eq? msg \'generate)\n             (set! state (rand-update state))\n             state)\n            ((eq? msg \'reset)\n             (lambda (new-val) (set! state new-val)))\n            (else (error "Unknown message" msg))))))\n\n(rand \'generate)\n(rand \'generate)\n((rand \'reset) 42)\n(rand \'generate)   ; same as first generate above' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.7', html: '<p>Consider the bank account objects created by <code>make-account</code>, with the password modification described in exercise 3.3. Suppose that our banking system requires the ability to make joint accounts. Define a procedure <code>make-joint</code> that accomplishes this. <code>make-joint</code> should take three arguments. The first is a password-protected account. The second argument must match the password with which the account was defined in order for the <code>make-joint</code> operation to proceed. The third argument is a new password. <code>make-joint</code> is to create an additional access to the original account using the new password.</p>', code: '(define (make-account balance password)\n  (define (withdraw amount)\n    (if (>= balance amount)\n        (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n        "Insufficient funds"))\n  (define (deposit amount)\n    (set! balance (+ balance amount)) balance)\n  (define (dispatch pw m)\n    (if (not (eq? pw password))\n        (lambda (x) "Incorrect password")\n        (cond ((eq? m \'withdraw) withdraw)\n              ((eq? m \'deposit) deposit)\n              ((eq? m \'balance) balance)\n              (else (error "Unknown request" m)))))\n  dispatch)\n\n(define (make-joint account old-pw new-pw)\n  (lambda (pw m)\n    (if (eq? pw new-pw)\n        (account old-pw m)\n        (lambda (x) "Incorrect password"))))\n\n(define peter-acc (make-account 100 \'open-sesame))\n(define paul-acc (make-joint peter-acc \'open-sesame \'rosebud))\n((peter-acc \'open-sesame \'withdraw) 10)  ; 90\n((paul-acc \'rosebud \'withdraw) 20)       ; 70' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.8', html: '<p>When we defined the evaluation model in section 1.1.3, we said that the first step in evaluating an expression is to evaluate its subexpressions. But we never specified the order in which the subexpressions should be evaluated (e.g., left to right or right to left). When we introduce assignment, the order in which the arguments to a procedure are evaluated can make a difference to the result. Define a simple procedure <code>f</code> such that evaluating <code>(+ (f 0) (f 1))</code> will return 0 if the arguments to <code>+</code> are evaluated left to right but will return 1 if the arguments are evaluated right to left.</p>', code: '; f must return 0 on first call, then preserve that.\n; Hint: use state so first argument seen determines result.\n(define f\n  (let ((state -1))\n    (lambda (x)\n      (if (= state -1)\n          (begin (set! state x) x)\n          state))))\n\n; Evaluation order determines the result:\n(+ (f 0) (f 1))   ; left-to-right: 0+0=0; right-to-left: 1+1=2\n; Reset and try again:\n' },

  // ── Section 3.2: The Environment Model ───────────────────────────────

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.9', html: '<p>In section 1.2.1 we used the substitution model to analyze two procedures for computing factorials, a recursive version</p><pre>(define (factorial n)\n  (if (= n 1) 1 (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))</pre><p>and an iterative version</p><pre>(define (factorial n) (fact-iter 1 1 n))\n(define (fact-iter product counter max-count)\n  (if (> counter max-count)\n      product\n      (fact-iter (* counter product)\n                 (+ counter 1)\n                 max-count)))</pre><p>Show the environment structures created by evaluating <code>(factorial 6)</code> using each version of the <code>factorial</code> procedure.</p>', code: '; This is a diagramming exercise.\n; Use the REPL to trace calls and understand the frames.\n(define (factorial n)\n  (if (= n 1) 1 (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n\n(factorial 6)  ; 720\n\n; Iterative version:\n(define (fact-iter product counter max-count)\n  (if (> counter max-count)\n      product\n      (fact-iter (* counter product) (+ counter 1) max-count)))\n(define (factorial-iter n) (fact-iter 1 1 n))\n(factorial-iter 6)  ; 720' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.10', html: '<p>In the <code>make-withdraw</code> procedure, the local variable <code>balance</code> is created as a parameter of <code>make-withdraw</code>. We could also create the local state variable explicitly, using <code>let</code>, as follows:</p><pre>(define (make-withdraw initial-amount)\n  (let ((balance initial-amount))\n    (lambda (amount)\n      (if (>= balance amount)\n          (begin (set! balance (- balance amount))\n                 balance)\n          "Insufficient funds"))))</pre><p>Recall from section 1.3.2 that <code>let</code> is syntactic sugar for a procedure call:</p><pre>(let ((&lt;var&gt; &lt;exp&gt;)) &lt;body&gt;)\n=  ((lambda (&lt;var&gt;) &lt;body&gt;) &lt;exp&gt;)</pre><p>Use this equivalence to explain in detail how environments work when <code>(make-withdraw 100)</code> is called. What environments are created?</p>', code: '(define (make-withdraw initial-amount)\n  (let ((balance initial-amount))\n    (lambda (amount)\n      (if (>= balance amount)\n          (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n          "Insufficient funds"))))\n\n(define W1 (make-withdraw 100))\n(W1 50)   ; 50\n(W1 30)   ; 20\n\n; Compare environment structure to the non-let version:\n(define (make-withdraw2 balance)\n  (lambda (amount)\n    (if (>= balance amount)\n        (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n        "Insufficient funds")))\n(define W2 (make-withdraw2 100))\n(W2 50)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.11', html: '<p>In section 3.2.3 we saw how the environment model described the behavior of procedures with local state. Now we have seen how internal definitions work. A typical message-passing procedure contains both of these aspects. Consider the bank account procedure of section 3.1.1:</p><pre>(define (make-account balance)\n  (define (withdraw amount) ...)\n  (define (deposit amount) ...)\n  (define (dispatch m) ...)\n  dispatch)</pre><p>Show the environment structure generated by the sequence of interactions</p><pre>(define acc (make-account 50))\n((acc \'deposit) 40)\n((acc \'withdraw) 60)</pre><p>Where is the local state for <code>acc</code> kept? Suppose we define a second account: <code>(define acc2 (make-account 100))</code>. How are the local states for the two accounts kept distinct? Which parts of the environment structure are shared between <code>acc</code> and <code>acc2</code>?</p>', code: '(define (make-account balance)\n  (define (withdraw amount)\n    (if (>= balance amount)\n        (begin (set! balance (- balance amount)) balance)\n        "Insufficient funds"))\n  (define (deposit amount)\n    (set! balance (+ balance amount))\n    balance)\n  (define (dispatch m)\n    (cond ((eq? m \'withdraw) withdraw)\n          ((eq? m \'deposit) deposit)\n          ((eq? m \'balance) balance)\n          (else (error "Unknown request" m))))\n  dispatch)\n\n(define acc (make-account 50))\n((acc \'deposit) 40)    ; 90\n((acc \'withdraw) 60)   ; 30\n\n(define acc2 (make-account 100))\n((acc2 \'withdraw) 40)  ; 60\n((acc \'balance))       ; 30 -- unaffected' },

  // ── Section 3.3: Modeling with Mutable Data ───────────────────────────

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.12', html: '<p>The following procedure for appending lists was introduced in section 2.2.1:</p><pre>(define (append x y)\n  (if (null? x) y (cons (car x) (append (cdr x) y))))</pre><p><code>append!</code> is a mutating variant of <code>append</code> that splices the lists together by modifying the final pair of <code>x</code> so that its <code>cdr</code> points to <code>y</code>:</p><pre>(define (append! x y)\n  (set-cdr! (last-pair x) y)\n  x)</pre><p>What are the missing values in the interactions below? Draw box-and-pointer diagrams showing the structures after each interaction.</p><pre>(define x (list \'a \'b))\n(define y (list \'c \'d))\n(define z (append x y))\nz                    ; (a b c d)\n(cdr x)              ; &lt;response&gt;\n(define w (append! x y))\nw                    ; (a b c d)\n(cdr x)              ; &lt;response&gt;</pre>', code: '(define (last-pair x)\n  (if (null? (cdr x)) x (last-pair (cdr x))))\n(define (append! x y)\n  (set-cdr! (last-pair x) y)\n  x)\n\n(define x (list \'a \'b))\n(define y (list \'c \'d))\n(define z (append x y))\nz           ; (a b c d)\n(cdr x)     ; (b) -- x unchanged by functional append\n(define w (append! x y))\nw           ; (a b c d)\n(cdr x)     ; (b c d) -- x is mutated!' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.13', html: '<p>Consider the following <code>make-cycle</code> procedure, which uses the <code>last-pair</code> procedure defined in exercise 3.12:</p><pre>(define (make-cycle x)\n  (set-cdr! (last-pair x) x)\n  x)</pre><p>Draw a box-and-pointer diagram that shows the structure <code>z</code> created by</p><pre>(define z (make-cycle (list \'a \'b \'c)))</pre><p>What happens if we try to compute <code>(last-pair z)</code>?</p>', code: '(define (last-pair x)\n  (if (null? (cdr x)) x (last-pair (cdr x))))\n(define (make-cycle x)\n  (set-cdr! (last-pair x) x)\n  x)\n\n(define z (make-cycle (list \'a \'b \'c)))\n; z is now a circular list.\n; (last-pair z) will loop forever -- do not run!\n; Instead, verify z is cyclic by checking:\n(eq? (cddr (cdr z)) z)   ; #t' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.14', html: '<p>The following procedure is quite useful, although obscure:</p><pre>(define (mystery x)\n  (define (loop x y)\n    (if (null? x)\n        y\n        (let ((temp (cdr x)))\n          (set-cdr! x y)\n          (loop temp x))))\n  (loop x \'()))</pre><p><code>loop</code> uses the temporary variable <code>temp</code> to hold the old value of the <code>cdr</code> of <code>x</code>, since the <code>set-cdr!</code> on the next line destroys the <code>cdr</code>. Explain what <code>mystery</code> does in general. Suppose <code>v</code> is defined by <code>(define v (list \'a \'b \'c \'d))</code>. Draw the box-and-pointer diagram that represents the list to which <code>v</code> is bound. Suppose that we now evaluate <code>(define w (mystery v))</code>. Draw box-and-pointer diagrams that show the structures <code>v</code> and <code>w</code> after evaluating this expression. What would be printed as the values of <code>v</code> and <code>w</code>?</p>', code: '(define (mystery x)\n  (define (loop x y)\n    (if (null? x)\n        y\n        (let ((temp (cdr x)))\n          (set-cdr! x y)\n          (loop temp x))))\n  (loop x \'()))\n\n(define v (list \'a \'b \'c \'d))\n(define w (mystery v))\n; mystery reverses x in-place\nw   ; (d c b a)\nv   ; (a) -- v\'s pair now points to \'()' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.15', html: '<p>Draw box-and-pointer diagrams to explain the effect of <code>set-to-wow!</code> on the structures <code>z1</code> and <code>z2</code> below.</p><pre>(define x (list \'a \'b))\n(define z1 (cons x x))\n(define z2 (cons (list \'a \'b) (list \'a \'b)))\n(define (set-to-wow! x)\n  (set-car! (car x) \'wow)\n  x)\n(set-to-wow! z1)\n(set-to-wow! z2)</pre>', code: '(define x (list \'a \'b))\n(define z1 (cons x x))\n(define z2 (cons (list \'a \'b) (list \'a \'b)))\n\n(define (set-to-wow! s)\n  (set-car! (car s) \'wow)\n  s)\n\n; z1 shares structure: both car and cdr point to same list x\n(set-to-wow! z1)   ; ((wow b) wow b) -- both halves change!\n\n; Rebuild z2 since we mutated z1\'s backing x already\n(define z2b (cons (list \'a \'b) (list \'a \'b)))\n(set-to-wow! z2b)  ; ((wow b) a b) -- only car half changes' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.16', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle decides to write a procedure to count the number of pairs in any list structure. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s easy,&rdquo; he reasons. &ldquo;The number of pairs in any structure is the number in the <code>car</code> plus the number in the <code>cdr</code> plus one more to count the current pair.&rdquo; So Ben writes the following procedure:</p><pre>(define (count-pairs x)\n  (if (not (pair? x))\n      0\n      (+ (count-pairs (car x))\n         (count-pairs (cdr x))\n         1)))</pre><p>Show that this procedure is not correct. In particular, draw box-and-pointer diagrams representing list structures made up of exactly three pairs for which Ben&rsquo;s procedure would return 3; return 4; return 7; never return at all.</p>', code: '(define (count-pairs x)\n  (if (not (pair? x))\n      0\n      (+ (count-pairs (car x))\n         (count-pairs (cdr x))\n         1)))\n\n; Returns 3 (correct): a normal 3-element list\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(count-pairs a)  ; 3\n\n; Returns 4: shared structure\n(define b (list 1 2))\n(define c (cons b b))       ; car and cdr share b\n(count-pairs c)  ; 4 (counts b twice)\n\n; Returns 7: more sharing\n(define d (list 1))\n(define e (cons d d))\n(define f (cons e e))\n(count-pairs f)  ; 7\n\n; Never returns: cycle\n; (define g (list 1 2 3))\n; (set-cdr! (cddr g) g)\n; (count-pairs g)  ; infinite loop' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.17', html: '<p>Devise a correct version of the <code>count-pairs</code> procedure of exercise 3.16 that returns the number of distinct pairs in any structure. (Hint: traverse the structure, maintaining an auxiliary data structure that is used to keep track of which pairs have already been counted.)</p>', code: '(define (count-pairs x)\n  (let ((seen \'()))\n    (define (count p)\n      (if (or (not (pair? p)) (memq p seen))\n          0\n          (begin\n            (set! seen (cons p seen))\n            (+ (count (car p))\n               (count (cdr p))\n               1))))\n    (count x)))\n\n; Test with shared structure:\n(define b (list 1 2))\n(define c (cons b b))\n(count-pairs c)   ; 3\n\n(define d (list 1))\n(define e (cons d d))\n(define f (cons e e))\n(count-pairs f)   ; 3' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.18', html: '<p>Write a procedure that examines a list and determines whether it contains a cycle, that is, whether a program that tried to find the end of the list by taking successive <code>cdr</code>s would go into an infinite loop. Exercise 3.13 constructed such lists.</p>', code: '(define (has-cycle? x)\n  (let ((seen \'()))\n    (define (detect p)\n      (cond ((not (pair? p)) #f)\n            ((memq p seen) #t)\n            (else\n             (set! seen (cons p seen))\n             (detect (cdr p)))))\n    (detect x)))\n\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(has-cycle? a)   ; #f\n\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(set-cdr! (cddr b) b)\n(has-cycle? b)   ; #t' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.19', html: '<p>Redo exercise 3.18 using an algorithm that takes only a constant amount of space. (This requires a very clever idea.)</p>', code: '; Floyd\'s tortoise-and-hare cycle detection:\n(define (has-cycle? x)\n  (define (race slow fast)\n    (cond ((not (pair? fast)) #f)\n          ((not (pair? (cdr fast))) #f)\n          ((eq? slow fast) #t)\n          (else (race (cdr slow) (cddr fast)))))\n  (if (not (pair? x)) #f\n      (race x (cdr x))))\n\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(has-cycle? a)   ; #f\n\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(set-cdr! (cddr b) b)\n(has-cycle? b)   ; #t' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.20', html: '<p>Draw environment diagrams to illustrate the evaluation of the sequence of expressions</p><pre>(define x (cons 1 2))\n(define z (cons x x))\n(set-car! (cdr z) 17)\n(car x)  ; 17</pre><p>using the procedural implementation of pairs given in section 3.3.1.</p>', code: '(define (cons x y)\n  (define (set-x! v) (set! x v))\n  (define (set-y! v) (set! y v))\n  (define (dispatch m)\n    (cond ((eq? m \'car) x)\n          ((eq? m \'cdr) y)\n          ((eq? m \'set-car!) set-x!)\n          ((eq? m \'set-cdr!) set-y!)\n          (else (error "Undefined operation" m))))\n  dispatch)\n(define (car z) (z \'car))\n(define (cdr z) (z \'cdr))\n(define (set-car! z v) ((z \'set-car!) v))\n(define (set-cdr! z v) ((z \'set-cdr!) v))\n\n(define x (cons 1 2))\n(define z (cons x x))\n(set-car! (cdr z) 17)\n(car x)   ; 17 -- x and (cdr z) are the same object' },

  // ── Section 3.3: Modeling with Mutable Data ─────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.21', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle decides to test the queue implementation. He types in the expressions and is surprised by the interpreter&rsquo;s response, because it shows the internal representation of the queue. Explain what Eva Lu Ator means by her reply: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s just the standard Lisp printer that doesn&rsquo;t know about queues.&rdquo; Define a procedure <code>print-queue</code> that prints just the items in the queue.</p>', code: '; (define (print-queue queue)\n;   (display (front-ptr queue))\n;   (newline))\n;\n; The printer shows the cons cell (front-ptr . rear-ptr),\n; but front-ptr alone IS the list of all elements.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.22', html: '<p>Instead of representing a queue as a pair of pointers, we can build a queue as a procedure with local state. The local state will consist of pointers to the beginning and the end of an ordinary list. Define the queue operations using this procedural representation.</p>', code: '(define (make-queue)\n  (let ((front-ptr \'())\n        (rear-ptr \'()))\n    (define (empty?) (null? front-ptr))\n    (define (insert! item)\n      (let ((new-pair (list item)))\n        (if (null? front-ptr)\n            (begin (set! front-ptr new-pair)\n                   (set! rear-ptr new-pair))\n            (begin (set-cdr! rear-ptr new-pair)\n                   (set! rear-ptr new-pair)))))\n    (define (delete!)\n      (if (null? front-ptr) (error "empty queue")\n          (begin (set! front-ptr (cdr front-ptr)))))\n    (define (front)\n      (if (null? front-ptr) (error "empty queue")\n          (car front-ptr)))\n    (define (dispatch m)\n      (cond ((eq? m \'empty?) (empty?))\n            ((eq? m \'insert!) insert!)\n            ((eq? m \'delete!) (delete!))\n            ((eq? m \'front) (front))))\n    dispatch))\n\n(define q (make-queue))\n((q \'insert!) \'a)\n((q \'insert!) \'b)\n(q \'front)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.23', html: '<p>A <em>deque</em> (&ldquo;double-ended queue&rdquo;) is a sequence in which items can be inserted and deleted at either the front or the rear. Operations on deques are the constructor <code>make-deque</code>, the predicate <code>empty-deque?</code>, selectors <code>front-deque</code> and <code>rear-deque</code>, and mutators <code>front-insert-deque!</code>, <code>front-delete-deque!</code>, <code>rear-insert-deque!</code>, <code>rear-delete-deque!</code>. Show how to represent deques using pairs, and give implementations of the operations. All operations should be accomplished in &Theta;(1) steps.</p>', code: '; A doubly-linked list node: (list item prev next)\n; Deque: (cons front-node rear-node)\n;\n; For O(1) rear-delete, need backward pointers.\n; Each node stores (item . (prev-ptr . next-ptr)).\n;\n; Implement make-deque, front/rear insert/delete.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.24', html: '<p>In the table implementations above, the keys are tested for equality using <code>equal?</code>. We might want a table where keys are tested using a different predicate. Generalize <code>make-table</code> to take as argument a <code>same-key?</code> procedure that will be used to test equality of keys.</p>', code: '(define (make-table same-key?)\n  (let ((local-table (list \'*table*)))\n    (define (lookup key)\n      (let ((record (assoc-custom key (cdr local-table))))\n        (if record (cdr record) #f)))\n    (define (assoc-custom key records)\n      (cond ((null? records) #f)\n            ((same-key? key (caar records)) (car records))\n            (else (assoc-custom key (cdr records)))))\n    (define (insert! key value)\n      (let ((record (assoc-custom key (cdr local-table))))\n        (if record (set-cdr! record value)\n            (set-cdr! local-table\n                      (cons (cons key value) (cdr local-table))))))\n    (define (dispatch m)\n      (cond ((eq? m \'lookup) lookup)\n            ((eq? m \'insert!) insert!)))\n    dispatch))\n\n(define t (make-table equal?))\n((t \'insert!) \'a 1)\n((t \'lookup) \'a)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.25', html: '<p>Generalizing one- and two-dimensional tables, show how to implement a table in which values are stored under an arbitrary number of keys and different values may be stored under different numbers of keys.</p>', code: '; Recursive table: each key leads to either a value\n; or a subtable. Use a list of keys for lookup/insert.\n;\n; (define (lookup keys table)\n;   (if (null? keys) (cdr table)\n;       (let ((subtable (assoc (car keys) (cdr table))))\n;         (if subtable\n;             (lookup (cdr keys) subtable)\n;             #f))))\n;\n; Insert similarly: create subtables as needed.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.26', html: '<p>To search a table as implemented above, one needs to scan through the list of records. This is basically the unordered list representation of section 2.3.3. For a large table, it may be more efficient to structure the table in a different manner. Describe a table implementation where the (key, value) records are organized using a binary tree, assuming that keys can be ordered.</p>', code: '; Replace the association list at each level with a\n; binary search tree. Each node: (key value left right).\n; lookup and insert use binary search: O(log n)\n; instead of O(n) for the list version.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.27', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>memo-fib</code> that computes Fibonacci numbers using memoization. Draw an environment diagram to explain how <code>memo-fib</code> computes the nth Fibonacci number in a number of steps proportional to n. Would the scheme still work if we had simply defined <code>memo-fib</code> to be <code>(memoize fib)</code>?</p>', code: '(define (memoize f)\n  (let ((table (list)))\n    (lambda (x)\n      (let ((result (assoc x table)))\n        (if result (cdr result)\n            (let ((val (f x)))\n              (set! table (cons (cons x val) table))\n              val))))))\n\n(define memo-fib\n  (memoize (lambda (n)\n    (cond ((= n 0) 0)\n          ((= n 1) 1)\n          (else (+ (memo-fib (- n 1))\n                   (memo-fib (- n 2))))))))\n\n(memo-fib 30)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.28', html: '<p>Define an or-gate as a primitive function box. Your <code>or-gate</code> constructor should be similar to <code>and-gate</code>.</p>', code: '; (define (or-gate a1 a2 output)\n;   (define (or-action-procedure)\n;     (let ((new-value (logical-or (get-signal a1) (get-signal a2))))\n;       (after-delay or-gate-delay\n;                    (lambda () (set-signal! output new-value)))))\n;   (add-action! a1 or-action-procedure)\n;   (add-action! a2 or-action-procedure)\n;   \'ok)\n;\n; (define (logical-or a b)\n;   (if (or (= a 1) (= b 1)) 1 0))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.29', html: '<p>Another way to construct an or-gate is as a compound digital logic device, built from and-gates and inverters. Define a procedure <code>or-gate</code> that accomplishes this using De Morgan&rsquo;s law: a OR b = NOT(NOT a AND NOT b).</p>', code: '; (define (or-gate a1 a2 output)\n;   (let ((c (make-wire))\n;         (d (make-wire))\n;         (e (make-wire)))\n;     (inverter a1 c)\n;     (inverter a2 d)\n;     (and-gate c d e)\n;     (inverter e output)\n;     \'ok))\n;\n; Delay = inverter-delay + and-gate-delay + inverter-delay' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.30', html: '<p>Write a procedure <code>ripple-carry-adder</code> that generates a ripple-carry adder for n-bit numbers. The inputs are lists of n wires A and B plus an initial carry-in C. The outputs are a list of n sum wires S and a carry-out wire.</p>', code: '; (define (ripple-carry-adder A B S C)\n;   (let ((c-in (make-wire)))\n;     (if (null? (cdr A))\n;         (begin (set-signal! c-in 0)\n;                (full-adder (car A) (car B) c-in (car S) C))\n;         (begin\n;           (full-adder (car A) (car B) c-in (car S) C)\n;           (ripple-carry-adder (cdr A) (cdr B) (cdr S) c-in)))))\n;\n; Delay for n bits = n * full-adder-delay' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.31', html: '<p>The internal procedure <code>accept-action-procedure!</code> defined in <code>make-wire</code> specifies that when a new action procedure is added to a wire, the procedure is immediately run. Explain why this initialization is necessary. In particular, trace through the half-adder example to show that the initial action run is essential for correct behavior.</p>', code: '; Without the initial run, inverter outputs would\n; start at 0 instead of 1 (the inverse of input 0).\n; The half-adder\'s D wire (inverter output) must start\n; at 1 for the and-gate to work correctly.\n;\n; Without initialization, the circuit would produce\n; wrong results until all gates have fired at least once.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.32', html: '<p>The procedures to be run during each time segment of the agenda are kept in a queue. Explain why the procedures must be run in the order they were added to the queue (FIFO). Trace the behavior of an and-gate whose inputs change from (0,1) to (1,0) in the same segment, and show that if we use a LIFO (stack) discipline, we get the wrong answer.</p>', code: '; Inputs change: a: 0->1, b: 1->0 (same time segment)\n; Two action procedures are queued:\n; 1. a changed: compute new output = 1 AND 1 = 1 (b still 1)\n; 2. b changed: compute new output = 1 AND 0 = 0\n;\n; FIFO: runs 1 then 2, final output = 0 (correct)\n; LIFO: runs 2 then 1, final output = 1 (wrong!)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.33', html: '<p>Using primitive multiplier, adder, and constant constraints, define a procedure <code>averager</code> that takes three connectors a, b, and c as inputs and establishes the constraint that the value of c is the average of the values of a and b.</p>', code: '; (define (averager a b c)\n;   (let ((sum (make-connector))\n;         (two (make-connector)))\n;     (adder a b sum)\n;     (constant 2 two)\n;     (multiplier c two sum)\n;     \'ok))\n;\n; c * 2 = a + b, so c = (a + b) / 2' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.34', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner wants to build a squarer, a constraint device with two terminals such that the value of connector b on the second terminal will always be the square of the value a on the first terminal. He proposes: <code>(define (squarer a b) (multiplier a a b))</code>. Explain the flaw in this idea.</p>', code: '; The multiplier constraint: m1 * m2 = product\n; If m1 and m2 are the SAME connector, setting b\n; won\'t determine a, because multiplier needs TWO\n; of three values to compute the third.\n; With a = a = ?, multiplier sees only one known value\n; (b) and two unknowns (both point to a), so it can\'t\n; compute a = sqrt(b).' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.35', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle tells Louis that one way to avoid the trouble in exercise 3.34 is to define a squarer as a new primitive constraint. Fill in the missing portions in Ben&rsquo;s outline for a procedure to implement such a constraint:</p>', code: '; (define (squarer a b)\n;   (define (process-new-value)\n;     (if (has-value? b)\n;         (if (< (get-value b) 0)\n;             (error "square less than 0")\n;             (set-value! a (sqrt (get-value b)) me))\n;         (if (has-value? a)\n;             (set-value! b (* (get-value a) (get-value a)) me))))\n;   (define (process-forget-value)\n;     (forget-value! a me)\n;     (forget-value! b me)\n;     (process-new-value))\n;   (define (me request)\n;     (cond ((eq? request \'I-have-a-value) (process-new-value))\n;           ((eq? request \'I-lost-my-value) (process-forget-value))))\n;   (connect a me)\n;   (connect b me)\n;   me)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.36', html: '<p>Suppose we evaluate the following sequence of expressions in the global environment:</p><pre>(define a (make-connector))\n(define b (make-connector))\n(set-value! a 10 \'user)</pre><p>Draw an environment diagram showing the state after these expressions have been evaluated.</p>', code: '; This is a diagram exercise.\n; a and b are each dispatch procedures with\n; local state (value, informant, constraints).\n; After set-value!, a\'s value is 10, informant is \'user.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.37', html: '<p>The <code>celsius-fahrenheit-converter</code> procedure is cumbersome when compared to a more expression-oriented style: <code>(define (celsius-fahrenheit-converter x) (c+ (c* (c/ (cv 9) (cv 5)) x) (cv 32)))</code>. Define <code>c+</code>, <code>c*</code>, <code>c/</code>, <code>cv</code> (constant value) in terms of the constraint primitives <code>adder</code>, <code>multiplier</code>, and <code>constant</code>.</p>', code: '; (define (c+ x y)\n;   (let ((z (make-connector)))\n;     (adder x y z) z))\n; (define (c* x y)\n;   (let ((z (make-connector)))\n;     (multiplier x y z) z))\n; (define (c/ x y)\n;   (let ((z (make-connector)))\n;     (multiplier z y x) z))\n; (define (cv val)\n;   (let ((z (make-connector)))\n;     (constant val z) z))' },

  // ── Section 3.4: Concurrency ────────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.38', html: '<p>Suppose that Peter, Paul, and Mary share a joint bank account that initially contains $100. Concurrently, Peter deposits $10, Paul withdraws $20, and Mary withdraws half the money. List all the different possible values for <code>balance</code> after the three transactions, assuming the banking system forces the three processes to run sequentially in some order.</p>', code: '; All orderings of (Peter, Paul, Mary):\n; P-Pa-M: 100+10=110, 110-20=90, 90/2=45\n; P-M-Pa: 100+10=110, 110/2=55, 55-20=35\n; Pa-P-M: 100-20=80, 80+10=90, 90/2=45\n; Pa-M-P: 100-20=80, 80/2=40, 40+10=50\n; M-P-Pa: 100/2=50, 50+10=60, 60-20=40\n; M-Pa-P: 100/2=50, 50-20=30, 30+10=40\n;\n; Possible values: 35, 40, 45, 50\n; With interleaving, other values are possible too.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.39', html: '<p>Which of the five possibilities in the parallel execution shown above remain if we instead serialize the two procedures?</p><pre>(define x 10)\n(define s (make-serializer))\n(parallel-execute (s (lambda () (set! x (* x x))))\n                  (s (lambda () (set! x (+ x 1)))))</pre>', code: '; With serialization, only sequential orderings remain:\n; (* x x) then (+ x 1): x=10, x=100, x=101\n; (+ x 1) then (* x x): x=10, x=11, x=121\n;\n; Only two possible results: 101 or 121' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.40', html: '<p>Give all possible values of x that can result from executing</p><pre>(define x 10)\n(parallel-execute (lambda () (set! x (* x x)))\n                  (lambda () (set! x (* x x x))))</pre><p>Which of these possibilities remain if we instead use serialized procedures?</p>', code: '; Without serialization: many interleavings possible.\n; x could be: 100, 1000, 10000, 100000, 1000000, ...\n;\n; With serialization (sequential):\n; (* x x) first: x=100, then (* 100 100 100)=1000000\n; (* x x x) first: x=1000, then (* 1000 1000)=1000000\n; Either way: x = 1000000' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.41', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle worries that it would be better to implement the bank account as follows (where even the <code>balance</code> accessor is serialized). Is this a reasonable worry? Explain.</p>', code: '; Serializing the balance read is unnecessary in this case.\n; Reading balance is a single memory access — atomic.\n; No interleaving within a single read can produce\n; an inconsistent value.\n;\n; However, if balance were computed from multiple\n; internal values, serialization would be needed.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.42', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle suggests creating serialized procedures at account creation time rather than creating them on each call to <code>deposit</code> or <code>withdraw</code>. Is this a safe change? What difference might it make?</p>', code: '; Safe: yes, both approaches are equivalent.\n; Pre-creating serialized procedures means the same\n; serialized wrapper is reused. The serializer still\n; ensures mutual exclusion.\n;\n; The difference: slightly more efficient since we\n; don\'t create a new serialized procedure on each call.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.43', html: '<p>Suppose that the balances in three accounts start out as $10, $20, and $30, and that multiple processes run, exchanging the balances in the accounts. Argue that if the processes are run sequentially, after any number of concurrent exchanges, the account balances should be $10, $20, and $30 in some order. Draw a timing diagram to show how this condition can be violated if the exchanges are implemented using the first version of the account-exchange program.</p>', code: '; Sequential exchanges are permutations: they just\n; rearrange values, so {10,20,30} is preserved.\n;\n; Without proper serialization, interleaved reads\n; and writes can lose updates. For example, if two\n; exchanges both read the same account balance before\n; either writes, the final state may have duplicate\n; values like {10,20,20} — sum is no longer 60.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.44', html: '<p>Consider the problem of transferring an amount from one account to another. Ben Bitdiddle claims that this can be accomplished with the following procedure, even with multiple people concurrently transferring money among multiple accounts, using any account mechanism that serializes deposit and withdrawal transactions:</p><pre>(define (transfer from-account to-account amount)\n  (from-account \'withdraw amount)\n  (to-account \'deposit amount))</pre><p>Louis Reasoner claims there is a problem here analogous to the exchange problem. Is Louis right?</p>', code: '; Louis is wrong. Transfer differs from exchange because\n; transfer doesn\'t need to read a balance and compute\n; a difference. It just withdraws a fixed amount and\n; deposits a fixed amount.\n;\n; The exchange problem arises because the difference\n; depends on reading both balances — a non-atomic\n; read-compute-write sequence.\n; Transfer is two independent serialized operations.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.45', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner thinks the bank-account system with <code>serialized-exchange</code> is overly complex. He suggests that deposits and withdrawals should no longer be individually serialized, since the exchange procedure is now serialized. Is Louis right? What would happen if <code>serialized-exchange</code> tried to use the account&rsquo;s own serializer for the entire exchange?</p>', code: '; Louis is wrong. If the account\'s serializer is used\n; for the exchange AND for individual deposits/withdrawals,\n; then when exchange calls withdraw (which is serialized\n; with the same serializer), it will deadlock — the\n; exchange already holds the serializer lock, and\n; withdraw tries to acquire it again.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.46', html: '<p>Suppose we implement <code>test-and-set!</code> using an ordinary procedure (not as an atomic, hardware-supported operation). Draw a timing diagram to show how the mutex implementation can fail by allowing two processes to acquire the mutex at the same time.</p>', code: '; Process A: (test-and-set! cell)\n;   reads cell = false\n; Process B: (test-and-set! cell)   [interleave here]\n;   reads cell = false\n; Process A: sets cell = true, returns false (acquired!)\n; Process B: sets cell = true, returns false (acquired!)\n;\n; Both processes think they have the mutex.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.47', html: '<p>A semaphore (of size n) is a generalization of a mutex. Like a mutex, a semaphore supports acquire and release operations, but it is more general in that up to n processes can acquire it concurrently.</p><p>a. Give an implementation in terms of mutexes.<br>b. Give an implementation in terms of atomic <code>test-and-set!</code> operations.</p>', code: '; a. Using a mutex to protect a counter:\n; (define (make-semaphore n)\n;   (let ((count 0) (mutex (make-mutex)))\n;     (define (acquire)\n;       (mutex \'acquire)\n;       (if (< count n)\n;           (begin (set! count (+ count 1)) (mutex \'release))\n;           (begin (mutex \'release) (acquire))))  ; retry\n;     (define (release)\n;       (mutex \'acquire)\n;       (set! count (- count 1))\n;       (mutex \'release))\n;     (define (dispatch m)\n;       (cond ((eq? m \'acquire) (acquire))\n;             ((eq? m \'release) (release))))\n;     dispatch))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.48', html: '<p>Explain in detail how the deadlock-avoidance method of ordering the accounts by number works to avoid deadlock in the exchange problem. Rewrite <code>serialized-exchange</code> to incorporate this idea.</p>', code: '; Deadlock occurs when A locks acct1 then waits for acct2,\n; while B locks acct2 then waits for acct1.\n;\n; If both always lock the lower-numbered account first,\n; this circular wait cannot occur.\n;\n; (define (serialized-exchange account1 account2)\n;   (let ((s1 (if (< (account1 \'id) (account2 \'id))\n;                 (account1 \'serializer)\n;                 (account2 \'serializer)))\n;         (s2 (if (< (account1 \'id) (account2 \'id))\n;                 (account2 \'serializer)\n;                 (account1 \'serializer))))\n;     ((s1 (s2 (lambda () (exchange account1 account2)))))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.49', html: '<p>Give a scenario where the deadlock-avoidance mechanism described above (ordering by account number) does not work. (Hint: the mechanism works when each process gets all the resources it needs in advance. Think about a situation where this is not the case.)</p>', code: '; If a process doesn\'t know which accounts it needs\n; until it has already acquired some locks, ordering\n; won\'t help. Example: process reads account A to\n; determine which other account B to access.\n; It locks A first, then discovers it needs B.\n; Another process may have locked B and needs A.\n; Since A was locked before knowing about B,\n; the ordering strategy couldn\'t be applied.' },

  // ── Section 3.5: Streams ────────────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.50', html: '<p>Complete the following definition, which generalizes <code>stream-map</code> to allow procedures that take multiple arguments, analogous to <code>map</code>.</p><pre>(define (stream-map proc . argstreams)\n  (if (⟨??⟩ (car argstreams))\n      the-empty-stream\n      (⟨??⟩\n       (apply proc (map ⟨??⟩ argstreams))\n       (apply stream-map\n              (cons proc (map ⟨??⟩ argstreams))))))</pre>', code: '; (define (stream-map proc . argstreams)\n;   (if (stream-null? (car argstreams))\n;       the-empty-stream\n;       (cons-stream\n;        (apply proc (map stream-car argstreams))\n;        (apply stream-map\n;               (cons proc (map stream-cdr argstreams))))))\n;\n; BiwaScheme doesn\'t have native streams,\n; but the concept generalizes map to lazy lists.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.51', html: '<p>In order to take a closer look at delayed evaluation, we will use the following procedure, which simply returns its argument after printing it:</p><pre>(define (show x) (display x) (newline) x)</pre><p>What does the interpreter print in response to evaluating each expression in the following sequence?</p><pre>(define x (stream-map show (stream-enumerate-interval 0 10)))\n(stream-ref x 5)\n(stream-ref x 7)</pre>', code: '; (stream-ref x 5) forces elements 0-5:\n; prints 1 2 3 4 5 (0 was already forced)\n; returns 5\n;\n; (stream-ref x 7) forces elements 6-7:\n; prints 6 7 (0-5 already memoized)\n; returns 7\n;\n; Memoization means each element is computed only once.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.52', html: '<p>Consider the sequence of expressions</p><pre>(define sum 0)\n(define (accum x) (set! sum (+ x sum)) sum)\n(define seq (stream-map accum (stream-enumerate-interval 1 20)))\n(define y (stream-filter even? seq))\n(define z (stream-filter (lambda (x) (= (remainder x 5) 0)) seq))\n(stream-ref y 7)\n(display-stream z)</pre><p>What is the value of <code>sum</code> after each of the above expressions is evaluated? What would these values be if we had implemented <code>delay</code> without memoization?</p>', code: '; With memoization:\n; After define seq: sum=1 (only first element forced)\n; After define y: sum=6 (forces until first even: 1,3,6)\n; After define z: sum=10 (forces until first %5=0: 10)\n; After stream-ref y 7: sum=136\n; After display-stream z: sum=210\n;\n; Without memoization, sum would be different because\n; each access would re-evaluate and re-accumulate.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.53', html: '<p>Without running the program, describe the elements of the stream defined by <code>(define s (cons-stream 1 (add-streams s s)))</code>.</p>', code: '; s = 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, ...\n; Each element is double the previous: powers of 2.\n; s(n) = 2^n' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.54', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>mul-streams</code>, analogous to <code>add-streams</code>. Use this together with the stream of integers to complete the following definition of the stream whose nth element (counting from 0) is n + 1 factorial:</p><pre>(define factorials (cons-stream 1 (mul-streams ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩)))</pre>', code: '; (define (mul-streams s1 s2)\n;   (stream-map * s1 s2))\n;\n; (define factorials\n;   (cons-stream 1 (mul-streams (stream-cdr integers) factorials)))\n;\n; factorials = 1, 1*2=2, 2*3=6, 6*4=24, ...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.55', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>partial-sums</code> that takes as argument a stream S and returns the stream whose elements are S₀, S₀ + S₁, S₀ + S₁ + S₂, &hellip;</p>', code: '; (define (partial-sums s)\n;   (cons-stream (stream-car s)\n;                (add-streams (stream-cdr s)\n;                             (partial-sums s))))\n;\n; (partial-sums integers) = 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, ...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.56', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>merge</code> and use it to define the stream of all positive integers with no prime factors other than 2, 3, or 5 (the <em>Hamming numbers</em> or <em>regular numbers</em>): 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, &hellip;</p>', code: '; (define (merge s1 s2)\n;   (cond ((stream-null? s1) s2)\n;         ((stream-null? s2) s1)\n;         (else\n;          (let ((s1car (stream-car s1))\n;                (s2car (stream-car s2)))\n;            (cond ((< s1car s2car)\n;                   (cons-stream s1car (merge (stream-cdr s1) s2)))\n;                  ((> s1car s2car)\n;                   (cons-stream s2car (merge s1 (stream-cdr s2))))\n;                  (else\n;                   (cons-stream s1car\n;                     (merge (stream-cdr s1) (stream-cdr s2)))))))))\n;\n; (define S (cons-stream 1\n;   (merge (scale-stream S 2)\n;          (merge (scale-stream S 3)\n;                 (scale-stream S 5)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.57', html: '<p>How many additions are performed when we compute the nth Fibonacci number using the definition of <code>fibs</code> based on the <code>add-streams</code> procedure? Show that the number of additions would be exponentially greater if we had implemented <code>(delay ⟨exp⟩)</code> simply as <code>(lambda () ⟨exp⟩)</code> without using memoization.</p>', code: '; With memoization: n-1 additions (linear).\n; Each fib(k) is computed once and cached.\n;\n; Without memoization: exponential.\n; Computing fib(n) requires fib(n-1) and fib(n-2),\n; each of which recomputes from scratch,\n; producing the same tree recursion as naive fib.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.58', html: '<p>Give an interpretation of the stream computed by the following procedure:</p><pre>(define (expand num den radix)\n  (cons-stream\n   (quotient (* num radix) den)\n   (expand (remainder (* num radix) den) den radix)))</pre><p>What are the successive elements produced by <code>(expand 1 7 10)</code>? <code>(expand 3 8 10)</code>?</p>', code: '; expand computes the digits of num/den in the given radix.\n;\n; (expand 1 7 10) = 1/7 in decimal = 1, 4, 2, 8, 5, 7, ...\n; (expand 3 8 10) = 3/8 in decimal = 3, 7, 5, 0, 0, 0, ...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.59', html: '<p>a. Define a procedure <code>integrate-series</code> that takes a stream a₀, a₁, a₂, &hellip; representing a power series and returns the stream a₀, a₁/2, a₂/3, &hellip; of coefficients of the integral of the series (omitting the constant term).</p><p>b. Use this to define the streams for e<sup>x</sup>, sine, and cosine as power series.</p>', code: '; (define (integrate-series s)\n;   (stream-map / s integers))\n;\n; (define exp-series\n;   (cons-stream 1 (integrate-series exp-series)))\n;\n; (define cosine-series\n;   (cons-stream 1 (integrate-series (scale-stream sine-series -1))))\n; (define sine-series\n;   (cons-stream 0 (integrate-series cosine-series)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.60', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>mul-series</code> for multiplying two power series.</p>', code: '; (define (mul-series s1 s2)\n;   (cons-stream (* (stream-car s1) (stream-car s2))\n;     (add-streams (scale-stream (stream-cdr s2) (stream-car s1))\n;                  (mul-series (stream-cdr s1) s2))))\n;\n; Verify: (add-streams (mul-series sine-series sine-series)\n;                      (mul-series cosine-series cosine-series))\n; should be 1, 0, 0, 0, ... (sin^2 + cos^2 = 1)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.61', html: '<p>Define a procedure <code>invert-unit-series</code> that computes 1/S for a unit power series S (one whose constant term is 1).</p>', code: '; (define (invert-unit-series s)\n;   (cons-stream 1\n;     (scale-stream\n;       (mul-series (stream-cdr s)\n;                   (invert-unit-series s))\n;       -1)))\n;\n; If S = 1 + S_r, then 1/S = 1 - S_r * (1/S)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.62', html: '<p>Use the results of exercises 3.60 and 3.61 to define a procedure <code>div-series</code> that divides two power series. <code>div-series</code> should work for any two series, provided that the denominator series begins with a nonzero constant term. Use <code>div-series</code> to compute the power series for tangent.</p>', code: '; (define (div-series s1 s2)\n;   (let ((c (stream-car s2)))\n;     (if (= c 0) (error "denominator has zero constant term")\n;         (mul-series s1\n;                     (invert-unit-series\n;                       (scale-stream s2 (/ 1 c)))))))\n;\n; (define tangent-series (div-series sine-series cosine-series))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.63', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner asks why we didn&rsquo;t define <code>sqrt-stream</code> as</p><pre>(define (sqrt-stream x)\n  (cons-stream 1.0\n    (stream-map (lambda (guess) (sqrt-improve guess x))\n                (sqrt-stream x))))</pre><p>instead of using the local variable <code>guesses</code>. Alyssa P. Hacker replies that this version is considerably less efficient because it performs redundant computation. Explain. Would Louis&rsquo;s version be any different if <code>delay</code> used memoization?</p>', code: '; Louis\'s version creates a NEW stream on each recursive\n; call. Even with memoization, each call to sqrt-stream\n; returns a distinct stream object, so previously computed\n; values are not shared.\n;\n; The original uses a self-referencing local variable,\n; so the same stream is reused and memoization works.\n;\n; Without memoization, both versions would be equally bad.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.64', html: '<p>Write a procedure <code>stream-limit</code> that takes a stream and a number (the tolerance). It should examine the stream until it finds two successive elements that differ by less than the tolerance, and return the second of the two elements.</p>', code: '; (define (stream-limit s tolerance)\n;   (let ((s0 (stream-car s))\n;         (s1 (stream-car (stream-cdr s))))\n;     (if (< (abs (- s0 s1)) tolerance)\n;         s1\n;         (stream-limit (stream-cdr s) tolerance))))\n;\n; (define (sqrt x tolerance)\n;   (stream-limit (sqrt-stream x) tolerance))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.65', html: '<p>Use the series ln 2 = 1 &minus; 1/2 + 1/3 &minus; 1/4 + &hellip; to compute three sequences of approximations to the natural logarithm of 2, one using the stream of partial sums, one using the Euler sequence accelerator, and one using the tableau of accelerated sequences. How rapidly do these sequences converge?</p>', code: '; (define (ln2-summands n)\n;   (cons-stream (/ 1.0 n)\n;                (stream-map - (ln2-summands (+ n 1)))))\n; (define ln2-stream (partial-sums (ln2-summands 1)))\n;\n; Direct partial sums: very slow convergence\n; Euler accelerated: much faster\n; Tableau (super-accelerated): extremely fast\n;\n; ln 2 = 0.693147...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.66', html: '<p>Examine the stream <code>(pairs integers integers)</code>. Can you make any general comments about the order in which the pairs are placed into the stream? How many pairs precede the pair (1,100)? The pair (99,100)? The pair (100,100)?</p>', code: '; The interleaving pattern for (pairs integers integers):\n; (1,1) (1,2) (2,2) (1,3) (2,3) (1,4) (3,3) (1,5) ...\n;\n; Pair (1,n) appears at position ~2n.\n; Pair (2,n) appears at position ~4n.\n; Pair (i,j) for i<j appears at roughly 2^i * j.\n; Pair (n,n) appears at roughly 2^n.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.67', html: '<p>Modify the <code>pairs</code> procedure so that <code>(pairs integers integers)</code> will produce the stream of <em>all</em> pairs of integers (i,j) (without the condition i &le; j).</p>', code: '; (define (all-pairs s t)\n;   (cons-stream\n;    (list (stream-car s) (stream-car t))\n;    (interleave\n;     (interleave\n;      (stream-map (lambda (x) (list (stream-car s) x))\n;                  (stream-cdr t))\n;      (stream-map (lambda (x) (list x (stream-car t)))\n;                  (stream-cdr s)))\n;     (all-pairs (stream-cdr s) (stream-cdr t)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.68', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner thinks the <code>pairs</code> procedure can be simplified:</p><pre>(define (pairs s t)\n  (interleave\n   (stream-map (lambda (x) (list (stream-car s) x)) t)\n   (pairs (stream-cdr s) (stream-cdr t))))</pre><p>Does this work? Consider what happens if we evaluate <code>(pairs integers integers)</code>.</p>', code: '; This doesn\'t work! The call to pairs in the second\n; argument of interleave is NOT delayed — it evaluates\n; immediately, causing infinite recursion.\n;\n; The original uses cons-stream, which delays the\n; recursive call. Louis\'s version has no delay.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.69', html: '<p>Write a procedure <code>triples</code> that takes three infinite streams, S, T, and U, and produces the stream of triples (Sᵢ, Tⱼ, Uₖ) such that i &le; j &le; k. Use <code>triples</code> to generate the stream of all Pythagorean triples of positive integers, i.e., the triples (i, j, k) such that i &le; j and i² + j² = k².</p>', code: '; (define (triples s t u)\n;   (cons-stream\n;    (list (stream-car s) (stream-car t) (stream-car u))\n;    (interleave\n;     (stream-map (lambda (p) (cons (stream-car s) p))\n;                 (stream-cdr (pairs t u)))\n;     (triples (stream-cdr s) (stream-cdr t) (stream-cdr u)))))\n;\n; (define pythagorean-triples\n;   (stream-filter\n;    (lambda (t) (= (+ (* (car t) (car t))\n;                      (* (cadr t) (cadr t)))\n;                   (* (caddr t) (caddr t))))\n;    (triples integers integers integers)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.70', html: '<p>Generalize the <code>merge</code> and <code>pairs</code> procedures to produce a <code>merge-weighted</code> and <code>weighted-pairs</code> procedure, where the pairs are ordered according to a given weighting function.</p><p>a. Generate pairs ordered by i + j.<br>b. Generate pairs ordered by 2i + 3j + 5ij, filtering out those where i or j is divisible by 2, 3, or 5.</p>', code: '; (define (merge-weighted s1 s2 weight)\n;   (cond ((stream-null? s1) s2)\n;         ((stream-null? s2) s1)\n;         (else\n;          (let ((w1 (weight (stream-car s1)))\n;                (w2 (weight (stream-car s2))))\n;            (if (<= w1 w2)\n;                (cons-stream (stream-car s1)\n;                  (merge-weighted (stream-cdr s1) s2 weight))\n;                (cons-stream (stream-car s2)\n;                  (merge-weighted s1 (stream-cdr s2) weight)))))))\n;\n; (define (weighted-pairs s t weight)\n;   (cons-stream\n;    (list (stream-car s) (stream-car t))\n;    (merge-weighted\n;     (stream-map (lambda (x) (list (stream-car s) x))\n;                 (stream-cdr t))\n;     (weighted-pairs (stream-cdr s) (stream-cdr t) weight)\n;     weight)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.71', html: '<p>Numbers that can be expressed as the sum of two cubes in more than one way are sometimes called <em>Ramanujan numbers</em>, in honor of the mathematician who discovered 1729 = 1³ + 12³ = 9³ + 10³. Generate a stream of such numbers using weighted pairs ordered by sum of cubes.</p>', code: '; Weight function: i^3 + j^3\n; Look for consecutive pairs with equal weight.\n;\n; (define (cube-weight pair)\n;   (+ (expt (car pair) 3) (expt (cadr pair) 3)))\n;\n; First few Ramanujan numbers:\n; 1729, 4104, 13832, 20683, 32832, 39312, ...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.72', html: '<p>In a similar way to exercise 3.71, generate a stream of all numbers that can be written as the sum of two squares in three different ways.</p>', code: '; Weight function: i^2 + j^2\n; Look for three consecutive pairs with equal weight.\n;\n; Similar to 3.71 but checking for three matches\n; instead of two.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.73', html: '<p>We can model electrical circuits using streams to represent the values of currents or voltages at a sequence of times. Write a procedure <code>RC</code> that models an RC circuit. <code>RC</code> should take as inputs the values of R, C, and dt and should return a procedure that takes as inputs a stream representing the current i and an initial value for the voltage v₀ and produces as output the stream of voltages v.</p>', code: '; v = v0 + (1/C)∫i dt + R*i\n;\n; (define (RC R C dt)\n;   (lambda (i v0)\n;     (add-streams\n;      (scale-stream i R)\n;      (integral (scale-stream i (/ 1 C)) v0 dt))))\n;\n; (define RC1 (RC 5 1 0.5))\n; (RC1 ones 0) would give the voltage stream' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.74', html: '<p>Alyssa P. Hacker is designing a system to process signals. She wants to produce a stream of zero crossings (transitions from positive to negative or vice versa). Complete her definition:</p><pre>(define (make-zero-crossings input-stream last-value)\n  (cons-stream\n   (sign-change-detector (stream-car input-stream) last-value)\n   (make-zero-crossings (stream-cdr input-stream)\n                        (stream-car input-stream))))</pre><p>Alyssa abstracts this using <code>stream-map</code>. Complete her expression.</p>', code: '; (define zero-crossings\n;   (stream-map sign-change-detector\n;              sense-data\n;              (cons-stream 0 sense-data)))\n;\n; sign-change-detector returns +1, -1, or 0\n; based on whether value crossed zero.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.75', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner wants to smooth the input signal before extracting zero crossings, by averaging each value with the previous. His implementation has a bug. Explain and fix it.</p>', code: '; Louis\'s bug: he averages the current value with the\n; previous AVERAGED value instead of the previous raw value.\n;\n; Fix: pass both the last raw value and the last averaged\n; value, and compute avpt from current and last raw values.\n;\n; (define (make-zero-crossings input-stream last-value last-avpt)\n;   (let ((avpt (/ (+ (stream-car input-stream) last-value) 2)))\n;     (cons-stream (sign-change-detector avpt last-avpt)\n;       (make-zero-crossings (stream-cdr input-stream)\n;                            (stream-car input-stream)\n;                            avpt))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.76', html: '<p>Eva Lu Ator criticizes Louis&rsquo;s approach in exercise 3.75 for not being modular. Instead, she suggests first smoothing the stream, then finding zero crossings of the smoothed stream. Implement <code>smooth</code> and show how to use it with <code>make-zero-crossings</code>.</p>', code: '; (define (smooth s)\n;   (stream-map (lambda (a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n;              s\n;              (stream-cdr s)))\n;\n; (define zero-crossings\n;   (make-zero-crossings (smooth sense-data) 0))\n;\n; Clean separation: smooth is reusable,\n; zero-crossing detector unchanged.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.77', html: '<p>The <code>integral</code> procedure used above was analogous to the &ldquo;implicit&rdquo; definition of the infinite stream of integers in section 3.5.2. Give a definition of <code>integral</code> that is more like the &ldquo;implicit&rdquo; definition and can handle delayed integrand arguments for solving differential equations.</p>', code: '; (define (integral delayed-integrand initial-value dt)\n;   (cons-stream initial-value\n;     (let ((integrand (force delayed-integrand)))\n;       (if (stream-null? integrand)\n;           the-empty-stream\n;           (integral (delay (stream-cdr integrand))\n;                     (+ (* dt (stream-car integrand))\n;                        initial-value)\n;                     dt)))))\n;\n; Now (define y (integral (delay dy) 1 0.001))\n; works with mutually referential streams.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.78', html: '<p>Consider the problem of generating the solution to the second-order differential equation d²y/dt² = a(dy/dt) + by. Write a procedure <code>solve-2nd</code> that takes as arguments the constants a, b, dt, and the initial values y₀ and dy₀.</p>', code: '; (define (solve-2nd a b dt y0 dy0)\n;   (define y (integral (delay dy) y0 dt))\n;   (define dy (integral (delay ddy) dy0 dt))\n;   (define ddy (add-streams (scale-stream dy a)\n;                            (scale-stream y b)))\n;   y)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.79', html: '<p>Generalize the <code>solve-2nd</code> procedure of exercise 3.78 so that it can be used to solve general second-order differential equations d²y/dt² = f(dy/dt, y).</p>', code: '; (define (solve-2nd f dt y0 dy0)\n;   (define y (integral (delay dy) y0 dt))\n;   (define dy (integral (delay ddy) dy0 dt))\n;   (define ddy (stream-map f dy y))\n;   y)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.80', html: '<p>A series RLC circuit consists of a resistor, a capacitor, and an inductor connected in series. Model this circuit as a procedure <code>RLC</code> that takes R, L, C, and dt as arguments and returns a procedure that takes initial values of vC₀ and iL₀ and produces a pair of streams of capacitor voltage and inductor current.</p>', code: '; (define (RLC R L C dt)\n;   (lambda (vC0 iL0)\n;     (define vC (integral (delay dvC) vC0 dt))\n;     (define iL (integral (delay diL) iL0 dt))\n;     (define dvC (scale-stream iL (/ -1 C)))\n;     (define diL (add-streams (scale-stream vC (/ 1 L))\n;                              (scale-stream iL (/ (- R) L))))\n;     (cons vC iL)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.81', html: '<p>Redo exercise 3.6 (the random-number generator with reset) using streams. The procedure should take as input a stream of requests (either <code>generate</code> or <code>reset</code> with a new value) and produce a stream of random numbers.</p>', code: '; (define (rand-stream requests)\n;   (define (next request last)\n;     (cond ((eq? request \'generate) (rand-update last))\n;           ((and (pair? request) (eq? (car request) \'reset))\n;            (cdr request))\n;           (else (error "bad request"))))\n;   (define s\n;     (cons-stream initial-random\n;       (stream-map next requests s)))\n;   s)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '3.82', html: '<p>Redo exercise 3.5 (Monte Carlo integration) using streams. The stream version of <code>estimate-integral</code> should produce a stream of estimates of the integral based on a stream of randomly chosen points.</p>', code: '; (define (estimate-integral P x1 x2 y1 y2)\n;   (define (random-in-range low high)\n;     (+ low (* (random) (- high low))))\n;   (define trials\n;     (cons-stream\n;      (P (random-in-range x1 x2) (random-in-range y1 y2))\n;      trials))  ; infinite stream of trial results\n;   (define running\n;     (stream-map / (partial-sums (stream-map (lambda (x) (if x 1.0 0.0)) trials))\n;                  integers))\n;   (scale-stream running (* (- x2 x1) (- y2 y1))))\n;\n; Estimate pi using unit circle:\n; (stream-ref (estimate-integral\n;   (lambda (x y) (<= (+ (* x x) (* y y)) 1))\n;   -1.0 1.0 -1.0 1.0) 10000)' },

];

